/* =============================================================================
   FLIGHT OPERATIONS INTELLIGENCE 2015 — DAX MEASURE LIBRARY
   58 measures · 9 display folders · hosted in the #Measures table

     00 Parameters .............  2      05 Cancellation Analysis ..  6
     01 Base Counts ............  6      06 Time Intelligence ......  6
     02 Rate KPIs ..............  6      07 Ranking & Labels .......  7
     03 Delay Magnitude ........  8      08 Validation .............  4
     04 Delay Attribution ...... 13
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   BOUND TO THE LIVE MODEL, VERIFIED IN POWER BI DESKTOP 2026-08-16.

     #Measures            hosts every measure
     flights              raw CSV column names, plus the derived columns
                          Delay Band, Delay Recovery, Departure Hour
     airlines             IATA_CODE, AIRLINE            (AIRLINE = full name)
     airports             IATA_CODE, AIRPORT, CITY, STATE, COUNTRY,
                          LATITUDE, LONGITUDE
     cancellation_codes   CANCELLATION_REASON, CANCELLATION_DESCRIPTION
     'Date Table'         Date, Month Number, Year Month, ...

   NAMING POLICY — decided deliberately, do not drift from it
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This model keeps the RAW physical column names from the source CSVs,
   everywhere, including the dimensions. That is the convention. It is not an
   oversight and it does not need "fixing".

   For friendly labels in the report, set DISPLAY NAMES in Model view
   (select the column > Properties > Name). Display names change what the reader
   sees; they do not rebind DAX. Renaming in Power Query DOES rebind, and would
   silently break every measure in this file at the next refresh.

   ONE TRAP WORTH KNOWING
     flights[AIRLINE]   = the two-letter IATA code   ("DL")
     airlines[AIRLINE]  = the full carrier name      ("Delta Air Lines Inc.")
   Same column name, two different things, in a related pair of tables. Always
   qualify the table. For a chart axis you almost always want airlines[AIRLINE].

   SETUP
     The #Measures table already exists. Create each measure inside it and set
     its Display folder from the Properties pane.

   HOUSEKEEPING FOUND IN THE LIVE MODEL — worth resolving
     - Two measures on the flights table carry error icons: "% Canc…" and
       "% Delay…". Diagnose or delete them; a model that ships with broken
       measures teaches its readers to ignore warning icons.
     - Measures live in two places: #Measures and the flights table. Move them
       all into #Measures. A measure parked on a table implies it belongs to
       that table, which is never true.
     - There is a table named "Metric" whose purpose is undocumented. Identify
       it or remove it.
   ============================================================================= */


/* #############################################################################
   00 Parameters
   ############################################################################# */

Target OTP = 0.80
-- On-time target. US industry benchmark is 80%.
-- Upgrade path: Modeling > New parameter > Numeric range 0.70-0.95, so the
-- business can move the target on the page instead of filing a change request.

Min Flights For Ranking = 5000
-- Volume floor for Best/Worst rankings. Hawaiian flies 3,368 flights against
-- Delta's 352,114; ranking them on one axis without a floor produces
-- statistically meaningless winners. Expose it, document it, let the business
-- move it.


/* #############################################################################
   01 Base Counts
   ############################################################################# */

Total Flights = COUNTROWS( flights )
-- Format: Whole number, thousands separator · Baseline 1,949,742

On Time Flights  = CALCULATE( [Total Flights], flights[Flight Status] = "On Time" )
-- Baseline 1,525,904

Delayed Flights  = CALCULATE( [Total Flights], flights[Flight Status] = "Delayed" )
-- Baseline 390,262. Arrival delay >= 15 min, per US DOT.

Cancelled Flights = CALCULATE( [Total Flights], flights[Flight Status] = "Cancelled" )
-- Baseline 28,570

Diverted Flights  = CALCULATE( [Total Flights], flights[Flight Status] = "Diverted" )
-- Baseline 5,006

Completed Flights =
[Total Flights] - [Cancelled Flights] - [Diverted Flights]
-- Baseline 1,916,166
-- Cancelled and diverted rows carry a NULL ARRIVAL_DELAY — verified: the "N/A"
-- delay band holds exactly 33,576 rows = 28,570 + 5,006. They have no
-- punctuality outcome at all, so they cannot sit in a punctuality denominator.


/* #############################################################################
   02 Rate KPIs
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Two on-time rates, two names. Both are correct; they answer different
   questions. There is deliberately NO measure called simply "On Time %" —
   this project previously published three different values under that one name.
   ############################################################################# */

On Time % (Completed) =
DIVIDE( [On Time Flights], [Completed Flights] )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal · Baseline 79.6%
-- OPERATIONAL KPI. "Of the flights we operated, how many landed on time?"
-- Use for trending, hub comparison, the report headline.

On Time % (Scheduled) =
DIVIDE( [On Time Flights], [Total Flights] )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal · Baseline 78.3%
-- CUSTOMER KPI. "Of the flights a passenger could book, how many arrived on
-- time?" Use for carrier league tables — without it, an airline can raise its
-- score by cancelling its weakest flights. American Eagle cancels 4.84% of its
-- schedule against Delta's 0.35%; on the Completed basis that is invisible.

Delay Rate % =
DIVIDE( [Delayed Flights], [Completed Flights] )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal · Baseline 20.4%
-- INVARIANT: [On Time % (Completed)] + [Delay Rate %] = 100.0% exactly.

Cancellation Rate % =
DIVIDE( [Cancelled Flights], [Total Flights] )
-- Format: Percentage, 2 decimals · Baseline 1.47%
-- Denominator is TOTAL on purpose. A cancellation is a broken promise, measured
-- against what was promised. A delay is a property of a flight that operated,
-- measured against what operated. Mixing the two is the most common error in
-- aviation reporting.

Diverted Rate % =
DIVIDE( [Diverted Flights], [Total Flights] )
-- Format: Percentage, 2 decimals · Baseline 0.26%

Disruption Rate % =
DIVIDE( [Delayed Flights] + [Cancelled Flights] + [Diverted Flights],
        [Total Flights] )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal · Baseline 21.7% (423,838 / 1,949,742 = 21.74%)
-- The single number for an executive: what share of the published schedule did
-- not go to plan.


/* #############################################################################
   03 Delay Magnitude
   ############################################################################# */

Avg Arrival Delay = AVERAGE( flights[ARRIVAL_DELAY] )
-- Format: Decimal 1 place, suffix " min" · Baseline 5.8 min
-- Strongly right-skewed. Never show it without the median beside it.

Median Arrival Delay = MEDIAN( flights[ARRIVAL_DELAY] )
-- Baseline -4 min
-- The gap between -4 and +5.8 is the story: most flights arrive early, and a
-- small tail of severe delays drags the mean positive.
-- PERFORMANCE: the most expensive measure here. Card only — never in a matrix
-- broken down by airline x month x airport.

Avg Delay (Delayed Only) =
CALCULATE( AVERAGE( flights[ARRIVAL_DELAY] ), flights[Flight Status] = "Delayed" )
-- The honest answer to "when we are late, how late are we?"

Total Positive Delay Minutes =
CALCULATE( SUM( flights[ARRIVAL_DELAY] ), flights[ARRIVAL_DELAY] > 0 )
-- Format: Whole number, thousands separator
-- NOT comparable to [Total Attributed Delay Min]: this counts every minute of
-- positive delay including flights 1-14 min late, which carry no attribution.
-- The two will never reconcile, which is exactly why they carry different
-- names. Two measures called "total delay" that do not tie is the fastest way
-- to lose a stakeholder's trust.
-- The > 0 filter matters: a plain SUM lets early arrivals net off real delays,
-- and an aircraft landing 20 min early does not refund another's 20-min delay.

Max Arrival Delay = MAX( flights[ARRIVAL_DELAY] )
-- Baseline 1,593 min (26.5 hours)

Avg Departure Delay = AVERAGE( flights[DEPARTURE_DELAY] )
-- Baseline 10.9 min

Avg Delay Recovery = AVERAGE( flights[Delay Recovery] )
-- Positive = minutes made up in the air. Measures schedule padding plus how
-- hard the airline flies to claw back a late departure.
-- Do NOT derive this by subtracting [Avg Departure Delay] - [Avg Arrival Delay].
-- Those two means cover different row populations and the subtraction is
-- invalid; validate_dataset.py reports both so the size of the error is visible.

Recovery Rate % =
VAR Recovered = CALCULATE( [Total Flights], flights[Delay Recovery] > 0 )
RETURN DIVIDE( Recovered, [Completed Flights] )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal
-- Share of operated flights that arrived relatively earlier than they departed.


/* #############################################################################
   04 Delay Attribution
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   These five columns are populated ONLY when arrival delay >= 15 min — 390,262
   rows, 79.98% NULL. The NULLs are structural and deliberately preserved, so
   AVERAGE() divides by 390,262 automatically with no CALCULATE filter needed.
   Zero-filling them would understate every cause by roughly 5x.
   ############################################################################# */

Air System Delay Min    = SUM( flights[AIR_SYSTEM_DELAY] )      -- 4,696,308 | 21.1%
Security Delay Min      = SUM( flights[SECURITY_DELAY] )        --    25,717 |  0.1%
Airline Delay Min       = SUM( flights[AIRLINE_DELAY] )         -- 7,542,717 | 33.8%
Late Aircraft Delay Min = SUM( flights[LATE_AIRCRAFT_DELAY] )   -- 8,709,198 | 39.1%
Weather Delay Min       = SUM( flights[WEATHER_DELAY] )         -- 1,328,155 |  6.0%

Total Attributed Delay Min =
[Air System Delay Min] + [Security Delay Min] + [Airline Delay Min]
    + [Late Aircraft Delay Min] + [Weather Delay Min]
-- Baseline 22,302,095 min

Controllable Delay % =
DIVIDE( [Airline Delay Min] + [Late Aircraft Delay Min],
        [Total Attributed Delay Min] )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal · Baseline 72.9% (16,251,915 / 22,302,095)
-- THE HEADLINE FINDING. Airline delay is the carrier's own fault directly;
-- late-aircraft delay is that same fault propagating from a previous leg.
-- At 72.9%, delay here is a scheduling and turnaround problem, not weather.
-- That reframes the remediation conversation entirely.

Avg Attributed Delay per Delayed Flight =
DIVIDE( [Total Attributed Delay Min], [Delayed Flights] )
-- Baseline 57.1 min. Gives the attribution folder a per-flight scale an
-- executive can hold in their head.

Avg Air System Delay    = AVERAGE( flights[AIR_SYSTEM_DELAY] )
Avg Security Delay      = AVERAGE( flights[SECURITY_DELAY] )
Avg Airline Delay       = AVERAGE( flights[AIRLINE_DELAY] )
Avg Late Aircraft Delay = AVERAGE( flights[LATE_AIRCRAFT_DELAY] )
Avg Weather Delay       = AVERAGE( flights[WEATHER_DELAY] )


/* #############################################################################
   05 Cancellation Analysis
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   cancellation_codes[CANCELLATION_REASON] holds the CODE (A/B/C/D/N).
   cancellation_codes[CANCELLATION_DESCRIPTION] holds the text.
   The column name says "reason" but carries the code — a source-file quirk, not
   something to fix. Set a display name of "Cancellation Code" in Model view if
   it confuses report readers.
   ############################################################################# */

Weather Cancellations =
CALCULATE( [Cancelled Flights], cancellation_codes[CANCELLATION_REASON] = "B" )
-- Baseline 16,372

Carrier Cancellations =
CALCULATE( [Cancelled Flights], cancellation_codes[CANCELLATION_REASON] = "A" )
-- Baseline 8,084

Air System Cancellations =
CALCULATE( [Cancelled Flights], cancellation_codes[CANCELLATION_REASON] = "C" )
-- Baseline 4,112

Security Cancellations =
CALCULATE( [Cancelled Flights], cancellation_codes[CANCELLATION_REASON] = "D" )
-- Baseline 2. Too small to visualise; exists so the four codes reconcile to
-- 28,570 on the validation page.

Weather Cancellation % =
DIVIDE( [Weather Cancellations], [Cancelled Flights] )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal · Baseline 57.3% (16,372 / 28,570)
-- PUT THIS NEXT TO [Controllable Delay %] ON ONE PAGE. Weather causes 6.0% of
-- delay MINUTES but 57.3% of CANCELLATIONS. Airlines absorb bad weather by
-- delaying; when a delay can no longer absorb it, they cancel. Two levers, two
-- narratives, one slide.

Cancellations per 1000 Flights =
DIVIDE( [Cancelled Flights], [Total Flights] ) * 1000
-- Format: Decimal, 1 place · Baseline 14.7 per 1,000.
-- Easier for a stakeholder to hold than "1.47%".


/* #############################################################################
   06 Time Intelligence
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   October 2015 contains zero rows. Every MoM measure would show a false -100%
   collapse. Guard every time-series visual with [Is Comparable Period] = 1 or
   'Date Table'[Has Flight Data].
   ############################################################################# */

Flights PM =
CALCULATE( [Total Flights], DATEADD( 'Date Table'[Date], -1, MONTH ) )

Flights MoM % =
VAR Curr = [Total Flights]
VAR Prev = [Flights PM]
RETURN IF( NOT ISBLANK( Prev ) && NOT ISBLANK( Curr ), DIVIDE( Curr - Prev, Prev ) )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal
-- Guard BOTH sides. Guarding only the prior period still publishes a
-- real-looking -100.0% for October, where Curr is blank and Prev is
-- September's 172,409.

On Time % PM =
CALCULATE( [On Time % (Completed)], DATEADD( 'Date Table'[Date], -1, MONTH ) )

On Time % MoM (pp) =
VAR Curr = [On Time % (Completed)]
VAR Prev = [On Time % PM]
RETURN IF( NOT ISBLANK( Prev ) && NOT ISBLANK( Curr ), Curr - Prev )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal
-- The name carries "(pp)" because this is a difference in PERCENTAGE POINTS,
-- not a percentage change. Labelling a 3.0pp move as "+3%" is a classic error.

Rolling 3M On Time % =
CALCULATE( [On Time % (Completed)],
    DATESINPERIOD( 'Date Table'[Date], MAX( 'Date Table'[Date] ), -3, MONTH ) )
-- Format: Percentage, 1 decimal
-- Must be CALCULATE, not AVERAGEX. DATESINPERIOD returns a table of individual
-- DATES, so AVERAGEX would iterate ~90 days and return the UNWEIGHTED mean of
-- 90 daily rates — a quiet Tuesday with 4,000 flights counting the same as a
-- Friday with 7,000. CALCULATE recomputes the ratio over the whole window, so
-- it is correctly volume-weighted.

Is Comparable Period =
IF( [Total Flights] > 0 && [Flights PM] > 0, 1, 0 )
-- Drop into the Filters pane (= 1) on every MoM visual so the October gap never
-- reaches a stakeholder's eye as a real business event.


/* #############################################################################
   07 Ranking & Dynamic Labels
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   NOTE the qualified references: airlines[AIRLINE] is the full carrier name,
   which is what belongs on a chart axis. flights[AIRLINE] is the IATA code.
   ############################################################################# */

Airline Rank by On Time =
IF( ISINSCOPE( airlines[AIRLINE] ),
    RANKX( ALLSELECTED( airlines[AIRLINE] ),
           [On Time % (Completed)], , DESC, DENSE ) )
-- TWO GUARDS, EACH DOING A DIFFERENT JOB:
--
-- ALLSELECTED, not ALL: with ALL, a user who filters to five airlines still
-- sees ranks out of fourteen, which reads as a bug to them.
--
-- ISINSCOPE, not NOT ISBLANK: on a table's total row there is no airline in
-- scope, but [On Time % (Completed)] still evaluates — to the overall 79.6% —
-- so a blank check does NOT suppress it and the total row renders "1".
-- ISINSCOPE asks the right question: is this row actually about an airline?
-- (Credit where due: this guard came from the existing model, not from the
-- first draft of this library.)
--
-- If a visual displays a different airlines column, this collapses to 1 on
-- every row — use ALLSELECTED( airlines ) to be display-column agnostic.

Airport Rank by On Time =
IF( ISINSCOPE( airports[AIRPORT] ),
    RANKX( ALLSELECTED( airports[AIRPORT] ),
           [On Time % (Completed)], , DESC, DENSE ) )

Best Airline =
VAR Ranked =
    FILTER(
        ADDCOLUMNS( ALLSELECTED( airlines[AIRLINE] ),
            "@OTP", [On Time % (Completed)],
            "@Vol", [Total Flights] ),
        NOT ISBLANK( [@OTP] ) && [@Vol] >= [Min Flights For Ranking] )
RETURN MAXX( TOPN( 1, Ranked, [@OTP], DESC ), airlines[AIRLINE] )
-- Returns a NAME for a card visual.

Worst Airline =
VAR Ranked =
    FILTER(
        ADDCOLUMNS( ALLSELECTED( airlines[AIRLINE] ),
            "@OTP", [On Time % (Completed)],
            "@Vol", [Total Flights] ),
        NOT ISBLANK( [@OTP] ) && [@Vol] >= [Min Flights For Ranking] )
RETURN MINX( TOPN( 1, Ranked, [@OTP], ASC ), airlines[AIRLINE] )
-- The NOT ISBLANK guard is load-bearing. BLANK sorts BELOW every real value in
-- an ascending TOPN, so without it any airline excluded by a slicer is reported
-- to the executive as the worst performing airline. That defect survives
-- testing and fails in a board meeting.

Top Delay Cause =
VAR Causes =
    DATATABLE( "Cause", STRING,
        { {"Late Aircraft"}, {"Airline"}, {"Air System"},
          {"Weather"}, {"Security"} } )
VAR Minutes =
    ADDCOLUMNS( Causes, "@Min",
        SWITCH( [Cause],
            "Late Aircraft", [Late Aircraft Delay Min],
            "Airline",       [Airline Delay Min],
            "Air System",    [Air System Delay Min],
            "Weather",       [Weather Delay Min],
            "Security",      [Security Delay Min] ) )
RETURN MAXX( TOPN( 1, Minutes, [@Min], DESC ), [Cause] )
-- Dynamic card naming the dominant driver for whatever the user has sliced to.
-- Re-ranks live as they filter by airport, month or carrier. Evaluates five
-- measures per row — fine on a card, do not put it in a matrix.

KPI Status Colour =
VAR Actual = [On Time % (Completed)]
RETURN SWITCH( TRUE(),
    ISBLANK( Actual ),             "#546077",
    Actual >= [Target OTP],        "#2E7D32",
    Actual >= [Target OTP] - 0.05, "#F9A825",
    "#C62828" )
-- Apply via Conditional formatting > Font colour > Format style: Field value.
-- BLANK renders neutral grey rather than alarming red, so October reads as
-- "no data" instead of "catastrophic month".

Data Coverage Note =
VAR MonthsWithData =
    CALCULATE( DISTINCTCOUNT( 'Date Table'[Year Month] ),
        FILTER( ALL( 'Date Table' ), [Total Flights] > 0 ) )
VAR FirstDate = CALCULATE( MIN( flights[Flight Date] ), ALL( flights ) )
VAR LastDate  = CALCULATE( MAX( flights[Flight Date] ), ALL( flights ) )
RETURN
    "Coverage: " & FORMAT( FirstDate, "MMM yyyy" ) & " - "
        & FORMAT( LastDate, "MMM yyyy" ) & " | " & MonthsWithData
        & " months with data | October 2015 is absent from the source extract."
-- Derived, not hardcoded: a hardcoded caveat goes stale at the first refresh,
-- and a stale caveat is worse than none. Place on every page. Never let a
-- stakeholder discover a data gap themselves in the middle of a meeting.
-- If 'Date Table'[Year Month] does not exist in your model, substitute the
-- equivalent year-month column.


/* #############################################################################
   08 Validation
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Put these on a hidden validation page. All four must read exactly 0 after
   every refresh. Wire them into the release checklist, not into memory.
   ############################################################################# */

Check Status Sum =
[Total Flights] - ( [On Time Flights] + [Delayed Flights]
                  + [Cancelled Flights] + [Diverted Flights] )

Check Rate Sum =
ROUND( [On Time % (Completed)] + [Delay Rate %] - 1, 6 )

Check Cancel Codes =
[Cancelled Flights] - ( [Weather Cancellations] + [Carrier Cancellations]
                      + [Air System Cancellations] + [Security Cancellations] )

Check Attribution =
[Total Attributed Delay Min] - ( [Air System Delay Min] + [Security Delay Min]
    + [Airline Delay Min] + [Late Aircraft Delay Min] + [Weather Delay Min] )


/* =============================================================================
   VALIDATION PAGE — expected values, single total row, no grouping

     Total Flights ................ 1,949,742
     On Time Flights .............. 1,525,904
     Delayed Flights ..............   390,262
     Cancelled Flights ............    28,570
     Diverted Flights .............     5,006
     Completed Flights ............ 1,916,166
     On Time % (Completed) ........     79.6%
     On Time % (Scheduled) ........     78.3%
     Delay Rate % .................     20.4%
     Cancellation Rate % ..........     1.47%
     Disruption Rate % ............     21.7%
     Total Attributed Delay Min ... 22,302,095
     Controllable Delay % .........     72.9%
     Weather Cancellation % .......     57.3%
     Check Status Sum .............         0
     Check Rate Sum ...............         0
     Check Cancel Codes ...........         0
     Check Attribution ............         0

   Every figure above was recomputed from the raw CSV by
   scripts/validate_dataset.py and passed. They are measured, not asserted.

   IF A NUMBER IS WRONG, it is almost always one of three causes:

     1. Flight Status built with > 15 instead of >= 15.
        Symptom: On Time exactly 14,206 too high, Delayed 14,206 too low.
     2. Cancelled/diverted rows left inside a punctuality denominator.
        Symptom: 78.3% where 79.6% was expected, or the reverse.
     3. The five delay-reason columns zero-filled instead of left NULL.
        Symptom: every AVERAGE-based attribution measure drops by ~80%.

   IF A MEASURE WILL NOT EVALUATE AT ALL, the cause is a name that has drifted.
   Check the table and column against the live model before changing the DAX —
   the model is the source of truth, not any document describing it. That
   mistake is what produced docs/REVIEW-CORRECTION.md.
   ============================================================================= */
